\details{
The full LBA is a race between one accumulator per response option, with the
winner determining both the choice and the RT. With no choice to model there
is nothing to race, so this is the single-accumulator version and the RT is
just that one accumulator's finishing time. All the RT variability comes from
across-trial variability in the start point and the drift rate, rather than
from moment-to-moment noise within the trial.

The threshold is written as an \emph{offset}, \code{b = sigmabias + boundary}, which is
the \code{B} parameterization of \code{DMC} and \code{EMC2} (\code{b = B + A}) rather than the
absolute threshold \code{rtdists} estimates. The threshold has to sit above the
highest possible starting point, and the offset makes \code{b > sigmabias} hold
automatically for any positive value instead of needing an order constraint
between two estimated parameters. See \code{\link[=cogmod_lba2]{cogmod_lba2()}} for the full note. The
cost is that \code{boundary} alone is not the quantity to read off a fitted model;
\code{boundary + sigmabias} is.

\code{sigma} is conventionally \strong{fixed to 1} rather than estimated, because the
evidence scale is arbitrary: multiplying \code{mu}, \code{sigma}, \code{sigmabias} and \code{boundary}
by a common constant leaves the decision time \code{(b - start) / drift} unchanged,
so only ratios are identified and one parameter must be pinned to fix the
scale. Fix it in the formula with \code{sigma = 1}.

As \code{sigmabias} shrinks toward zero the model reduces to the \emph{recinormal}, in
which \code{1 / (RT - ndt)} is normally distributed - the LATER model. That limit
is reached smoothly, which means the likelihood becomes \strong{flat} in
\code{sigmabias} as it approaches zero: once the start-point range is small enough,
making it smaller stops changing the density. On a \code{softplus} link zero is at
minus infinity, so a flat prior there leaves the posterior improper, and the
symptom is a chain that wanders off rather than one that fails. Fitted without
priors on the 4285-trial data in \code{vignette("rt_models")}, \code{sigmabias} for one
condition ran to \code{softplus(-10.4) = 3e-05} with \code{Rhat} 1.69 and an effective
sample size of 6.

\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} fences both \code{sigmabias} and \code{boundary} off for this reason -
the threshold is \code{b = sigmabias + boundary}, so the two share the ridge - in
the same way and for the same reason it fences off \code{ndt} and \code{poutlier}. Pass
\code{prior = cogmod_priors(f, df)}; the defaults are weak (\code{normal(0, 1)} on the
softplus scale, so a start-point range of roughly 0.3 to 1.3) and are meant to
be replaced rather than relied on if you know more.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}} and \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} work here
too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for the full account.

Note that \code{posterior_epred()} is not available: the decision time has no
finite mean, because \code{E[1 / drift]} diverges for a normal truncated at zero.
}
